course: dictionary
lesson: 11829
title: Informal words and tone
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some words are for everyday talk, not for formal writing. Blabbermouth is one of them. It is an informal noun, so you will hear it among friends and family more than in a school report or a letter. «Don't tell him, he's a blabbermouth.»
explain: A blabbermouth is a person who talks too much, especially someone who tells secrets. Look at the clues in this sentence: «Don't tell Sam about the surprise party. He's a blabbermouth.» The speaker is worried that Sam will spill the secret, so blabbermouth must mean a person who cannot keep quiet.
explain: Words also carry a feeling. «Such a blabbermouth!» is usually said with a smile or a tease, not as a serious insult. It tells you the speaker is a bit annoyed but not really angry. When you read a word like this, ask yourself: is the speaker cross, joking, or worried?
fact: Blabbermouth is an informal noun, so it belongs in everyday talk and stories.
fact: A blabbermouth is a person who talks too much, especially about secrets.
fact: «Don't be a blabbermouth» is a common phrase that means don't tell other people what you were told in private.
fact: Blabbermouth is mildly rude or teasing, not a very serious insult.
fact: Context clues, like a secret being spilled, can help you work out what an unknown word means.
exercise: «Don't tell Sam about the surprise party. He's a blabbermouth.» What is a blabbermouth? | *A person who talks too much and tells secrets | A person who cannot speak at all | A person who loves surprise parties | A person who keeps every secret safe
exercise: «Ruby told the whole class what Mum had bought.» Why might someone call Ruby a blabbermouth here? | *She told everyone a secret | She bought a present for Mum | She sang a song in assembly | She forgot her homework
exercise: Copy the word that means a person who talks too much about secrets. | party | *blabbermouth | secret | surprise
test: Blabbermouth is called informal. What does that tell you? | It means a very quiet person | It is the longest word in the dictionary | *It is a word for everyday talk, not formal writing | It is a word only scientists use
test: «Such a blabbermouth!» is said about a friend who blurted out a surprise. What feeling does the speaker most likely have? | Great praise and admiration | Deep anger that lasts for weeks | *Mild teasing, not very serious | Fear of the dark
exam: Which sentence shows that a character cannot keep a secret? | *«Jo whispered the secret to everyone in the lunch queue.» | «The sun was warm on the grass.» | «Mia kept her promise and said nothing.» | «He counted slowly to ten.»
